{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 as a local caching DNS resolver used by system applications.","status":"satisfied","summary":"NetworkManager persistently selects 127.0.0.1, dnsmasq listens only on IPv4/IPv6 loopback port 53, and repeated queries directly demonstrate caching through forwarded-versus-locally-answered counters. The same configuration and behavior recovered after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ad6b2fcc1e6b4f4a","cmd-360934242da24255","cmd-a42ab6264815427a","cmd-8d74520b751546f4","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Preserve normal external name resolution for applications on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"External application resolution worked before the change, after applications were switched to the local resolver, and after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9ea47fd382d544f5","cmd-a42ab6264815427a","cmd-8d74520b751546f4"]}],"confidence":1.0,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Installing dnsmasq and bind-utils, writing its configuration, enabling the service, changing NetworkManager DNS, issuing bounded cache/statistics probes, and scheduling the required restart each had a clear implementation or evidence role. Retained changes support the task. The final lifecycle snapshot shows healthy package state, no failed services, only the intended loopback DNS listeners, and no executor-created temporary residue; per-boot systemd-private directory changes are normal restart effects.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5a779714cd7f46c9","cmd-28a1409ced1b4aee","cmd-88c6aa4eabb44f00","cmd-ad6b2fcc1e6b4f4a","cmd-360934242da24255","cmd-a42ab6264815427a","cmd-2425a23a0e834e6b","cmd-8d74520b751546f4","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the restart was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, a different boot ID appeared afterward, and resolver service, persistent configuration, external lookup, and cache behavior recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":[]}
